---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import MCQuiz from '../../components/MCQuiz.astro';
---

<Layout title="What's missing from this hand-written form?" description="Practice card. A raw HTML form that looks complete; three things form_with would have added.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Course
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Reading the Source · Card 5</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">What's missing from this hand-written form?</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">A developer reaches for raw HTML instead of <code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-base">form_with</code>. The form posts, but the update doesn't happen. Three pieces are missing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <Icon name="lucide:search" class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500 inline-block" aria-hidden="true" /> The form
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 italic mb-3">For an existing Post (id: 42). The intent is "update the title and body."</p>
          <div class="border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-gray-50/40">
            <CodeBlock code={`<form action="/posts/42" method="post">
  <label>Title</label>
  <input type="text" name="title">

  <label>Body</label>
  <textarea name="body"></textarea>

  <input type="submit" value="Update Post">
</form>`} language="html" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The question</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Which set of additions would <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">form_with model: @post</code> have inserted into this form?</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <MCQuiz
          options={[
            {
              label: 'A',
              text: 'A jQuery dependency, an async fetch wrapper, and a loading spinner.',
              explanation: '<code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">form_with</code> is server-rendered HTML. No JavaScript dependency. The form posts the regular way.',
            },
            {
              label: 'B',
              text: 'A CSRF token, a hidden <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">_method</code> field, and field names like <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">post[title]</code>.',
              explanation: 'All three are essential. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">_method=patch</code> tells <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">Rack::MethodOverride</code> to rewrite the POST as a PATCH so the right route matches. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">authenticity_token</code> is what <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">protect_from_forgery</code> checks. The <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">post[...]</code> namespacing is what makes <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">params[:post][:title]</code> work on the server.',
              correct: true,
            },
            {
              label: 'C',
              text: 'A CORS preflight header, a session ID cookie, and a hidden user ID field.',
              explanation: 'CORS isn\'t form-related (it\'s for cross-origin XHR). The session cookie is sent automatically by the browser, not inserted into the form body. The user ID isn\'t typically in the form; it comes from the session.',
            },
            {
              label: 'D',
              text: 'Server-side validation messages, ARIA attributes, and Turbo data attributes.',
              explanation: 'Validation messages are rendered after submission, not built into the form HTML. ARIA and Turbo attributes are nice-to-have but aren\'t what makes the basic request work.',
            },
          ]}
          principle={`<p>An HTML form can only do GET or POST. Rails uses three hidden details to bridge the gap to its full REST surface:</p><ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 ml-4"><li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">_method</code> — read by <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">Rack::MethodOverride</code> middleware. Without it, an update form ends up matching the create route.</li><li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">authenticity_token</code> — read by <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">protect_from_forgery</code>. Without it, the request 422s.</li><li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">name="post[title]"</code> — read by Rack\'s nested-params parser. Without it, your <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">params.require(:post)</code> raises.</li></ul><p>Hand-written forms break for one of these three reasons more often than any other. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">form_with</code> exists so you don\'t have to remember.</p>`}
          theoryHref="/lessons/source-form-with"
          theoryLabel="Reading the Source · Card 7 — How form_with builds an HTML form"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-before-action-halt" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Card 4</a>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Back to Course →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
